// SPLIT: valid-type-orders 1/2
// P03.23 review (R1, R5): the check for a type that holds itself by value
// gives the same answer whatever the order of the declarations, and always
// ends (a copy, a Clone() and a zero value of each type follow each program;
// they ran forever, or crashed, on the shapes below). Every program is here
// in the order it was found in and in the reverse order, and each matches
// what Go reports (go build, 1.25): the same programs are accepted and
// rejected, and no instantiation cycle is reported where Go reports the
// recursion (R5). Some shapes were found by a differential fuzzer against Go
// (.scratch/fuzz.py in the review); the issue65711 ones are the programs of
// go.dev/issue/65711, where Go's cache of valid types was wrong. A ring of
// right-hand sides is named as Go's walk names it, in both orders (R22).
// `valid-*` programs report nothing (valid-alias-of-literal only the type
// errors that show the types are what Go makes of them). The review round 2
// shapes (r12-r14): a by-value cycle found while another type's walk had
// failed already is still reported by the walk of its own type, in either
// order; a cycle through a generic alias is an instantiation cycle, and the
// walks after it end; a defined type named in an alias's right-hand side sees
// the alias's type. Found by the fuzzer while fixing those: a by-value cycle
// through instances expanded before their generic type was found to be on an
// instantiation cycle, the Option-argument walk over such instances, and a
// ring of defined types through a type declared after an alias; round 3: the
// zero-value query over such instances (it answered from the declaration,
// which held a by-value cycle the expanded types did not, and overflowed the
// stack), and the pinned walk over instances that grow by value.
package main
